From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: tyn <tyn@localhost>
Date: Sun, 22 Nov 2026 06:48:00 -0800
Subject: [PATCH] tyn: built-in Bink 2 decoding (libbink2) behind FFmpegPlayer

FFmpeg reads Bink 2 containers but has no Bink 2 video decoder, so games
whose movies are Bink 2 (STEINS;GATE Steam: the world line change nixie
tubes, the title, the time leap background, alpha overlays) could not play
them from the original files.

- vendor/libbink2 (LGPL-2.1+, from the FFmpeg Bink 2 patches; README.impacto
  lists the local changes): KB2g+ alpha plane decoding added after FFmpeg
  bink2g.c. Built as a static C99 library with -ffp-contract=off so it rounds
  like the reference decoder.
- Bink2Player: decodes on a worker thread into a small queue, converts the
  full range YUV of Bink to the limited range the engine shaders expect,
  writes alpha movies in the engine's stacked layout (picture on top, alpha
  as luma below), decodes Bink Audio 2 up front and plays it on the movie
  channel. The movie clock is frame time kept in step with the sound while it
  plays (the sound track can end before the picture; its position then goes
  back to 0).
- FFmpegPlayer forwards Play/Stop/Update/Render/ReinitAudio to Bink2Player
  for files starting with "KB2" and sets SF_MOVIE_DRAWWAIT / SW_MOVIEFRAME
  like its own path.

Generic; can go upstream.

Verified: on PC against the game's bink2w32.dll (24 Bink 2 movies of
STEINS;GATE Steam; picture matches; imv004 frame 9, a special key frame,
cannot be decoded and shows transparent). On MuMu (Android 15 x86_64
emulator, not a real device) with the original Steam files and the 2018
Chinese patch: title (KB2j, alpha) loops, the alpha overlays play, the world
line change movie imv007 plays all 211 frames with sound.

